>> title: Chintan Broth, Instant Pot
>> category: Soups
>> tags: chicken, pork, japanese, stock, ramen, instant pot, pressure cooker
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> aka: instant pot chintan, pressure cooker ramen broth, pressure cooker chintan, clear ramen broth, torigara broth, 清湯, the broth under shoyu and shio ramen
>> servings: 8
>> dish: chintan-broth
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> pairs-with: shoyu-ramen, shio-ramen, miso-ramen
>> time: 3 hr
>> slack: narrow — the parboil is the only skimming you get: a broth that goes under the lid dirty comes out dirty
>> washing-up: the Instant Pot, a colander to rinse the bones in, a fine sieve, the straining cloth, a jug to settle the broth in, a jar for the chicken fat
>> keeps: 4 days — it stays clear cold and sets softly; lift the fat cap before it goes back on the heat

>> step: Clear is the one thing a sealed pot is worst at. This trades some of it for four hours.
The stovetop version is four hours of never letting it boil, and clarity is the whole point of it.

>> step: parboil 10 min, rinse every piece
Cover @chicken carcasses{2 1/2%lb}(1.1 kg; backs and frames, wing tips left on), @chicken wings{1%lb}(450 g) and @pork neck bones{3/4%lb}(340 g) with @cold water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in the #Instant Pot{}, bring up to a boil on Sauté and ~parboil{10%min}, then drain and rinse every piece under the tap and scrub the inner pot.

>> step: cook at high pressure 60 min
Return @&(~1)rinsed bones{} to the pot with @water{2 1/2%qt}(2.4 L), lock the lid and ~pressure cook{60%min} at high pressure.

>> step: wait out the full natural release 30 min — never the valve
Let @&(~1)pot{} ~natural release{30%min} until the pin drops by itself.

>> step: steep the aromatics and kombu 30 min in the residual heat
Drop @yellow onion{1%large}(halved), @fresh ginger{2%in}(sliced), @scallion greens{4%stalks} and @kombu{1%piece}(4 in) into @&(~1)broth{} and ~steep{30%min} in the residual heat with the lid resting on top.

>> step: strain through cloth, stand 20 min, lift the fat off and keep it
Strain @&(~1)broth{} through a fine sieve lined with a cloth, let it ~stand{20%min} and lift the fat off the top. That fat is the chicken fat the shoyu and shio bowls float on.
